  user_input {
    user_response: "# STEP 1 \342\200\224 Build the LittleSteps design system and UI foundation\n\nAct as a senior UI/UX designer and frontend architect.\n\nCreate a complete reusable design system for LittleSteps before building individual application pages.\n\n## Design direction\n\nThe design should feel:\n- Warm and welcoming.\n- Calm and reassuring.\n- Modern and premium.\n- Mother-friendly.\n- Simple enough for non-technical users.\n- Visually engaging without looking childish.\n\nAvoid making the platform look like a school management system or a complicated medical application.\n\n## Color palette\n\nUse this visual direction:\n\n- Primary: Soft sage green.\n- Secondary: Warm cream.\n- Background: Off-white.\n- Cards: White.\n- Accent: Muted peach.\n- Highlight: Soft lavender.\n- Text: Dark charcoal.\n- Secondary text: Muted grey.\n\nUse colors consistently. Avoid excessive gradients, saturated colors, and unnecessary shadows.\n\n## Typography\n\nUse a clean, readable font such as Inter, Nunito, or another suitable web font.\n\nEstablish:\n- Page heading styles.\n- Section heading styles.\n- Body text.\n- Supporting text.\n- Button text.\n- Form labels.\n- Small metadata.\n\nUse comfortable line heights and readable font sizes.\n\n## Component library\n\nBuild reusable components:\n\n1. Primary and secondary buttons.\n2. Input fields.\n3. Dropdowns.\n4. Date picker.\n5. Navigation bar.\n6. Sidebar.\n7. Mobile bottom navigation.\n8. Cards.\n9. Progress indicators.\n10. Badges.\n11. Tabs.\n12. Modal dialogs.\n13. Confirmation dialogs.\n14. Empty states.\n15. Loading skeletons.\n16. Toast notifications.\n17. Video player.\n18. Activity cards.\n19. Age-group selector.\n20. Reusable page headers.\n\n## Layout requirements\n\nDesktop:\n- Consistent maximum content width.\n- Clear navigation hierarchy.\n- Sidebar or top navigation where appropriate.\n- Comfortable spacing.\n- Consistent card alignment.\n\nMobile:\n- Responsive layout starting at 320px width.\n- Bottom navigation for primary parent screens.\n- Large touch targets.\n- No horizontal scrolling.\n- Forms should be easy to complete with one hand.\n\n## UI quality requirements\n\n- Follow WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility principles.\n- Provide visible keyboard focus.\n- Use semantic HTML.\n- Ensure readable text contrast.\n- Include hover, focus, disabled, loading, and error states.\n- Avoid unnecessary animation.\n- Respect reduced-motion preferences.\n\n## Implementation instructions\n\n1. Inspect the existing codebase.\n2. Implement design tokens.\n3. Create reusable components.\n4. Create a sample page demonstrating the design system.\n5. Verify responsive behavior.\n6. Add basic component tests where appropriate.\n\nDo not build the complete application yet.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA reusable design system with a polished sample page that can serve as the visual reference for every future LittleSteps screen.\n\nDo not proceed to Step 2 until the design system is implemented and reviewed."
    items {
      text: "# STEP 1 \342\200\224 Build the LittleSteps design system and UI foundation\n\nAct as a senior UI/UX designer and frontend architect.\n\nCreate a complete reusable design system for LittleSteps before building individual application pages.\n\n## Design direction\n\nThe design should feel:\n- Warm and welcoming.\n- Calm and reassuring.\n- Modern and premium.\n- Mother-friendly.\n- Simple enough for non-technical users.\n- Visually engaging without looking childish.\n\nAvoid making the platform look like a school management system or a complicated medical application.\n\n## Color palette\n\nUse this visual direction:\n\n- Primary: Soft sage green.\n- Secondary: Warm cream.\n- Background: Off-white.\n- Cards: White.\n- Accent: Muted peach.\n- Highlight: Soft lavender.\n- Text: Dark charcoal.\n- Secondary text: Muted grey.\n\nUse colors consistently. Avoid excessive gradients, saturated colors, and unnecessary shadows.\n\n## Typography\n\nUse a clean, readable font such as Inter, Nunito, or another suitable web font.\n\nEstablish:\n- Page heading styles.\n- Section heading styles.\n- Body text.\n- Supporting text.\n- Button text.\n- Form labels.\n- Small metadata.\n\nUse comfortable line heights and readable font sizes.\n\n## Component library\n\nBuild reusable components:\n\n1. Primary and secondary buttons.\n2. Input fields.\n3. Dropdowns.\n4. Date picker.\n5. Navigation bar.\n6. Sidebar.\n7. Mobile bottom navigation.\n8. Cards.\n9. Progress indicators.\n10. Badges.\n11. Tabs.\n12. Modal dialogs.\n13. Confirmation dialogs.\n14. Empty states.\n15. Loading skeletons.\n16. Toast notifications.\n17. Video player.\n18. Activity cards.\n19. Age-group selector.\n20. Reusable page headers.\n\n## Layout requirements\n\nDesktop:\n- Consistent maximum content width.\n- Clear navigation hierarchy.\n- Sidebar or top navigation where appropriate.\n- Comfortable spacing.\n- Consistent card alignment.\n\nMobile:\n- Responsive layout starting at 320px width.\n- Bottom navigation for primary parent screens.\n- Large touch targets.\n- No horizontal scrolling.\n- Forms should be easy to complete with one hand.\n\n## UI quality requirements\n\n- Follow WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility principles.\n- Provide visible keyboard focus.\n- Use semantic HTML.\n- Ensure readable text contrast.\n- Include hover, focus, disabled, loading, and error states.\n- Avoid unnecessary animation.\n- Respect reduced-motion preferences.\n\n## Implementation instructions\n\n1. Inspect the existing codebase.\n2. Implement design tokens.\n3. Create reusable components.\n4. Create a sample page demonstrating the design system.\n5. Verify responsive behavior.\n6. Add basic component tests where appropriate.\n\nDo not build the complete application yet.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA reusable design system with a polished sample page that can serve as the visual reference for every future LittleSteps screen.\n\nDo not proceed to Step 2 until the design system is implemented and reviewed."
    }
  }
}
