  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Understanding the Steps**\n\nI\'m focused on grasping the user\'s intent within the \"LittleSteps\" project. It\'s clear they want a step-by-step approach. This will influence how I structure the initial design system and UI foundation, ensuring it supports this sequential user flow effectively.\n\n\n**Defining the Core Design**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing the LittleSteps design system, starting with Step 1\'s specifications. The focus is pinpoint accuracy in the design tokens, ensuring they perfectly match the requested palette and typography. I\'ve already meticulously defined colors like soft sage green (`#446E5A`) and warm cream (`#F6F1E7`). I\'ve also done the contrast ratio analysis and calculated for the soft sage green, ensuring that our color choices meet WCAG 2.2 AA standards for accessibility.\n\n\n**Refining the Color Palette**\n\nI\'ve been solidifying the LittleSteps color palette with fresh additions. I\'ve introduced Muted Peach, Soft Lavender for highlights, and Dark Charcoal for text and Muted Grey for secondary text. I\'ve also added semantic states with Sage success, Ochre warning, and Crimson error. I\'m focusing on ensuring accessibility with 4.5:1 contrast ratios.\n\n**Selecting the Typography**\n\nI\'m now focusing on typography choices. For display and UI headings, I\'ve selected Nunito for its warmth and approachability. For body text, I\'m using Plus Jakarta Sans for its readability. I\'ve included IBM Plex Mono with tabular numbers for data representation.\n\n\n**Defining Typography Choices**\n\nI\'ve decided to keep `Nunito` for display and UI, paired with `Plus Jakarta Sans` for body text, both in the `index.html` file. I\'m focusing on defining reusable typography tokens and classes for all common elements: page headings, section headings, body text, supporting text, button text, form labels, and small metadata. This will streamline the design implementation and maintain consistency.\n\nI\'m now establishing 20 modular, reusable components inside `/src/design-system/` and exporting them from `/src/design-system/index.ts`. These include buttons with various styles and sizes, inputs with helper text and error states, custom accessible selects, and a clean date picker with a baby-age preview helper.\n\n\n**Listing the Components**\n\nI\'m now making a thorough list of reusable components and their core features. Specifically, I\'m focusing on defining the key elements like `Navbar`, `Sidebar`, `MobileBottomNav`, and `Card`, with their primary functionalities and accessibility considerations clearly outlined. This involves a precise component breakdown. I\'m taking the time to define their individual characteristics.\n\n\n**Expanding the Components**\n\nI\'m now detailing the characteristics of the newest components for the UI library. I\'ve been focusing on components such as `Badge` with several muted variants. I\'ve also incorporated accessible `Tabs` with keyboard navigation, a versatile `Modal` with responsive behavior, and a `ConfirmDialog` for safer user interactions. I\'ve also included `EmptyState`, `Skeleton` components, and `Toast` and `ToastProvider`.\n\n\n**Expanding the Components**\n\nI\'m now detailing the implementation of the remaining components for the UI library. I\'ve been focusing on components such as `VideoPlayer`, with HTML5 `<video>` support for demonstrations, and the privacy/retention metadata footer. The keyboard accessibility and non-downloadable feature is a key consideration. I\'ve also incorporated `ActivityCard`, an intuitive display for Montessori-inspired tasks. `AgeGroupSelector` with a responsive grid and ARIA semantics is also in progress. I\'m focusing on the `PageHeader`, a reusable title section.\n\n\n**Prioritizing the Showcase**\n\nI\'m now making the Design System Showcase the default view, fulfilling the user\'s immediate needs. I\'ll include an easily accessible preview toggle to explore the full app prototype without altering the existing code. I\'m focusing on showcasing all 20 components, color tokens, and the typography scale in an interactive way on this page, including the desktop sidebar and topbar layout, and mobile bottom nav and responsive preview.\n\n\n**Testing the Components**\n\nI\'m now focused on verifying the design system components with a lightweight testing strategy. My goal is to ensure semantic HTML, proper ARIA attributes, and correct states are implemented across all components. Specifically, I\'m using Node\'s built-in test runner (`node --test`) along with `tsx` and React 19 (`react-dom/server` with `renderToStaticMarkup`) to create efficient TypeScript component tests. This includes validating the markup for modals, confirm dialogs, tabs, the `AgeGroupSelector` with all age groups, and ensuring proper ARIA roles and token definitions.\n\n\n"
