  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Mapping Requirements Rigorously**\n\nI\'m deep in the weeds of Step 8, meticulously mapping out every requirement for the reviewer dashboard. The goal is a bulletproof architecture, so I\'m drilling down to a 4-level breakdown. I\'m prioritizing completeness and anticipating potential edge cases to ensure a robust and functional design. The focus now is nailing down the critical features and user interactions.\n\n\n**Refining Assessment Calculations**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the rubric rating scale upgrade. I need to make sure the `RubricAchievementLevel` incorporates \'met\', \'partially_met\', \'not_met\', and \'not_assessable\', while maintaining backward compatibility with existing data. The exact score calculation, `calculateInstructionMatchScore`, demands careful definition of `applicableWeightedPoints` and `earnedPoints`, including the \'Insufficient Evidence\' and \'Not Assessable\' scenarios.\n\n\n**Defining Assessment Logic**\n\nI\'ve completed the assessment calculation logic and am now verifying it against the database schema. Handling \'Not Assessable\' scenarios is key, ensuring we never produce a misleading percentage score. The goal is to update the SQLite schema in `server/db/schema.ts`, migrating the `review_rubric_scores` and `submission_reviews` tables safely while maintaining data integrity, including new \'not_met\', and \'not_assessable\' achieved levels. I\'m focusing on clean, idempotent migrations to accommodate existing seeded rows.\n\n\n**Constructing Audit History**\n\nI\'m now building the `review_audit_history` table, essential for tracking every review change. It\'s an immutable, append-only log that will hold every draft, publish, edit, and any \'insufficient evidence\' decisions. The table includes fields for `submission_id`, `review_id`, `actor_user_id`, `actor_role`, and various review details, ensuring a complete record of every action taken. I\'m focusing on its structure and its relationships with other tables for maximum data integrity.\n\n\n**Extending Audit Trails**\n\nI\'m now expanding the audit trail\'s scope to encompass every single review action. My current focus is to integrate every draft save, publication, update, and even \'not assessable\' markings into the `review_audit_history`. I\'m extending the existing `review_audit_history` schema. This involves capturing comprehensive version histories to ensure we can fully reconstruct the review process at any point in time. The goal is complete transparency and a robust audit log.\n\n**Refining Queue Functionality**\n\nI am now upgrading the `/api/v1/reviewer/queue` endpoint. The main focus is to return a summary with crucial KPIs, like total pending reviews, the number under review, completed reviews, and requests for resubmissions. Additionally, I\'m enhancing the queue itself to provide an array of `ReviewerQueueItem` instances. This includes new properties such as a privacy-minimized opaque reference, along with submission title, video source details, submission status, and reviewer status.\n\n\n**Constructing Queue Items**\n\nI\'m now integrating new properties into `ReviewerQueueItem` for the enhanced `/api/v1/reviewer/queue` endpoint. The aim is to create detailed, privacy-conscious queue items that offer all necessary details, including an identifier like `MEERA1` or `PAR-XXXXXX`. I am including submission details like `activityTitle`, `activityVersion`, `domainName`, `createdAt`, `ageGroupLabel`, and `ageGroupSlug`. I\'m also adding `reviewStatus` and a dynamically computed `priority` value based on queue age, along with assessment-related properties like `isAssessable`, `instructionMatchScorePct`, and `hasDraft`.\n\n\n**Defining Review Form Structure**\n\nI\'m now designing the user interface for the review form, integrating a weighted rubric with \'Met\', \'Partially Met\', \'Not Met\', and \'Not Assessable\' options. It\'s critical that the live score calculation is dynamic, displaying either a percentage or \'Not Assessable\' as appropriate. I\'m building in structured feedback fields for positive observations, areas to improve, and general comments. I am focusing on the actions now, the \'Save Draft\' and \'Submit\' actions.\n\n\n**Implementing Review Actions**\n\nI\'m now integrating critical reviewer actions, focusing on rich feedback and change requests. This means detailed notes and actionables, along with the ability to easily request a resubmission, logged neatly in the `review_audit_history`. I\'m also implementing the \"Not Assessable\" option, ensuring clarity and avoiding misleading scores when evidence is insufficient. This involves updating the review state and providing an explanation, which is also tracked in the audit trail. Finally, I\'m integrating the review history, making all versions and changes clearly visible, with a timestamp.\n\n\n\n**Enforcing Review Constraints**\n\nI\'m now implementing strict security rules. I\'m focusing on preventing edits to published reviews through robust server-side role checks on all review mutation routes. I\'m designing the user experience so the reviewer sees a clear, complete summary of the review, displaying instruction-match percentages or a \'Not Assessable\' badge with explanation, along with criteria-wise feedback, positive observations, areas to improve, reviewer comments, and the review date.\n\n\n"
