    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STEP 1 \342\200\224 Build the LittleSteps design system and UI foundation\n\nAct as a senior UI/UX designer and frontend architect.\n\nCreate a complete reusable design system for LittleSteps before building individual application pages.\n\n## Design direction\n\nThe design should feel:\n- Warm and welcoming.\n- Calm and reassuring.\n- Modern and premium.\n- Mother-friendly.\n- Simple enough for non-technical users.\n- Visually engaging without looking childish.\n\nAvoid making the platform look like a school management system or a complicated medical application.\n\n## Color palette\n\nUse this visual direction:\n\n- Primary: Soft sage green.\n- Secondary: Warm cream.\n- Background: Off-white.\n- Cards: White.\n- Accent: Muted peach.\n- Highlight: Soft lavender.\n- Text: Dark charcoal.\n- Secondary text: Muted grey.\n\nUse colors consistently. Avoid excessive gradients, saturated colors, and unnecessary shadows.\n\n## Typography\n\nUse a clean, readable font such as Inter, Nunito, or another suitable web font.\n\nEstablish:\n- Page heading styles.\n- Section heading styles.\n- Body text.\n- Supporting text.\n- Button text.\n- Form labels.\n- Small metadata.\n\nUse comfortable line heights and readable font sizes.\n\n## Component library\n\nBuild reusable components:\n\n1. Primary and secondary buttons.\n2. Input fields.\n3. Dropdowns.\n4. Date picker.\n5. Navigation bar.\n6. Sidebar.\n7. Mobile bottom navigation.\n8. Cards.\n9. Progress indicators.\n10. Badges.\n11. Tabs.\n12. Modal dialogs.\n13. Confirmation dialogs.\n14. Empty states.\n15. Loading skeletons.\n16. Toast notifications.\n17. Video player.\n18. Activity cards.\n19. Age-group selector.\n20. Reusable page headers.\n\n## Layout requirements\n\nDesktop:\n- Consistent maximum content width.\n- Clear navigation hierarchy.\n- Sidebar or top navigation where appropriate.\n- Comfortable spacing.\n- Consistent card alignment.\n\nMobile:\n- Responsive layout starting at 320px width.\n- Bottom navigation for primary parent screens.\n- Large touch targets.\n- No horizontal scrolling.\n- Forms should be easy to complete with one hand.\n\n## UI quality requirements\n\n- Follow WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility principles.\n- Provide visible keyboard focus.\n- Use semantic HTML.\n- Ensure readable text contrast.\n- Include hover, focus, disabled, loading, and error states.\n- Avoid unnecessary animation.\n- Respect reduced-motion preferences.\n\n## Implementation instructions\n\n1. Inspect the existing codebase.\n2. Implement design tokens.\n3. Create reusable components.\n4. Create a sample page demonstrating the design system.\n5. Verify responsive behavior.\n6. Add basic component tests where appropriate.\n\nDo not build the complete application yet.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA reusable design system with a polished sample page that can serve as the visual reference for every future LittleSteps screen.\n\nDo not proceed to Step 2 until the design system is implemented and reviewed."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STEP 2 \342\200\224 Build the LittleSteps public website\n\nBuild the public-facing website using the approved design system.\n\n## Required pages\n\n1. Home / Landing page\n2. About LittleSteps\n3. How It Works\n4. Our Approach\n5. Contact Us\n6. Privacy Policy\n7. Terms and Conditions\n8. Login\n9. Register\n\n## Landing page UI instructions\n\nCreate the following sections in order:\n\n### 1. Hero section\n- Main headline: \"Little moments. Big milestones.\"\n- Supporting text explaining how LittleSteps helps mothers support their baby\'s early development.\n- Primary CTA: \"Start Your Journey\"\n- Secondary CTA: \"Explore How It Works\"\n- Include a warm, high-quality mother-and-baby visual.\n- Keep the text short and emotionally relatable.\n\n### 2. How it works\nShow three simple steps:\n- Choose your baby\'s age.\n- Follow daily activities.\n- Learn, practice, and track your journey.\n\nUse illustrations or simple icons.\n\n### 3. Development areas\nShow six attractive cards representing the six development domains.\n\n### 4. Personalized learning journey\nExplain how activities are selected based on the baby\'s age.\n\n### 5. Activity demonstration\nShow an example activity card with an instructional video preview, duration, materials, and instructions.\n\n### 6. Progress and achievements\nExplain how parents can track completed activities.\n\n### 7. Safety and trust\nExplain that activities are designed with age appropriateness and safety guidance in mind. Do not make unverified medical or developmental claims.\n\n### 8. Final CTA\nInvite mothers to start their journey.\n\n### 9. Footer\nInclude navigation links, contact information, privacy, and terms.\n\n## UI rules\n\n- Use generous whitespace.\n- Use warm illustrations and authentic-looking photography.\n- Use rounded cards with subtle borders.\n- Keep the design premium and uncluttered.\n- Ensure all buttons navigate to real destinations.\n- Add responsive mobile layouts.\n\nDo not invent testimonials, statistics, certifications, or expert endorsements.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA complete responsive public website with working navigation and a polished landing page."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STEP 3 \342\200\224 Implement authentication and parent onboarding\n\nBuild the authentication and onboarding experience for LittleSteps.\n\n## Authentication pages\n\n1. Registration\n2. Login\n3. Forgot password\n4. Reset password\n5. Email verification\n6. Logout\n\nUse secure authentication appropriate to the existing architecture.\n\n## Parent onboarding journey\n\nAfter registration, guide the mother through a simple step-by-step onboarding process.\n\n### Screen 1: Welcome\nHeading: \"Let\'s get to know your little one.\"\n\nExplain that this information helps personalize activities.\n\n### Screen 2: Parent information\nCollect:\n- Parent\'s name.\n- Email.\n- Preferred language.\n- Optional profile photo.\n\n### Screen 3: Baby information\nCollect:\n- Baby\'s name or preferred nickname.\n- Date of birth.\n- Optional profile photo.\n- Relevant accessibility or activity preferences, if needed.\n\n### Screen 4: Development journey\nExplain the six development areas in simple language.\n\n### Screen 5: Personalization\nAllow the parent to select:\n- Preferred daily activity time.\n- Notification preference.\n- Preferred language.\n\n### Screen 6: Completion\nShow a welcoming success screen and take the parent to the dashboard.\n\n## UI requirements\n\n- Use a stepper to show onboarding progress.\n- Ask only necessary questions.\n- Use large input fields and clear labels.\n- Explain why information is being collected.\n- Allow users to go back and edit previous steps.\n- Show validation messages next to the relevant fields.\n- Make optional fields clearly optional.\n- Ensure the form works on mobile.\n\n## Security\n\n- Parents must only access their own profiles and children.\n- Validate all input on the server.\n- Do not expose passwords or sensitive information in logs.\n- Implement appropriate session management.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA fully functional authentication and onboarding flow connected to the application backend."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STEP 4 \342\200\224 Build the LittleSteps parent dashboard\n\nBuild the main dashboard that mothers see after logging in.\n\nThe dashboard must answer three questions immediately:\n\n1. What should I do with my baby today?\n2. How is my learning journey progressing?\n3. Where should I go next?\n\n## Desktop layout\n\nUse:\n- Left sidebar for navigation.\n- Top header with greeting, notifications, and profile.\n- Main content area with a comfortable maximum width.\n\n## Mobile layout\n\nUse:\n- Compact header.\n- Main content in a single column.\n- Fixed bottom navigation.\n- No desktop sidebar.\n- Large touch-friendly controls.\n\n## Dashboard sections\n\n### 1. Welcome area\nExample:\n\"Good morning, Sarah!\"\n\"Ready for today\'s little moments?\"\n\nDisplay the baby\'s name and age.\n\n### 2. Today\'s learning journey\nCreate a prominent card showing:\n- Today\'s activities completed.\n- Total activities.\n- Overall daily completion.\n- CTA: \"Continue Today\'s Journey\".\n\n### 3. Today\'s activities\nShow activity cards containing:\n- Activity title.\n- Development domain.\n- Estimated duration.\n- Age group.\n- Thumbnail.\n- Completion status.\n- CTA: \"Start Activity\".\n\n### 4. Weekly progress\nShow a simple visual chart of completed activities.\n\n### 5. Development areas\nShow six cards with individual progress indicators.\n\n### 6. Continue learning\nShow the last activity the parent viewed or started.\n\n### 7. Parenting tip\nShow one short, relevant tip.\n\n## Navigation\n\nPrimary navigation:\n- Today\n- My Plan\n- Activities\n- Progress\n\nSecondary navigation:\n- Parenting Guides\n- Notifications\n- Profile\n- Settings\n\n## UI behavior\n\n- Display loading skeletons.\n- Create meaningful empty states.\n- Show friendly error messages.\n- Ensure progress values come from actual application data.\n- Use realistic sample data only when clearly identified as demo data.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA responsive parent dashboard connected to the application data layer."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STEP 5 \342\200\224 Build the structured daily learning journey\n\nThis is the core feature of LittleSteps.\n\nCreate a structured daily plan based on the baby\'s age group.\n\n## Main page: My Plan\n\nDisplay:\n- Current baby age.\n- Current learning week.\n- Today\'s date.\n- Daily completion progress.\n- List of activities for the day.\n\n## Activity distribution\n\nActivities should cover the six development domains:\n\n1. Cognitive.\n2. Language and communication.\n3. Physical.\n4. Social and emotional.\n5. Sensory.\n6. Early independence and everyday skills.\n\nDo not force every domain into every day if it results in an unrealistic workload. The content plan should be age-appropriate and reviewed by qualified child-development experts.\n\n## Activity card design\n\nEach activity card must show:\n- Activity title.\n- Development domain.\n- Estimated duration.\n- Difficulty or preparation level, if relevant.\n- Required materials.\n- Completion status.\n- CTA to view activity.\n\nUse visual differences for:\n- Not started.\n- In progress.\n- Completed.\n- Submitted for review.\n- Reviewed.\n\n## Journey experience\n\nThe parent should be able to:\n- View today\'s activities.\n- View upcoming activities.\n- View previously completed activities.\n- Open activity details.\n- Resume an unfinished activity.\n\n## Important business rules\n\n- Activities must be filtered by the baby\'s age group.\n- Daily plans must not duplicate activities unexpectedly.\n- Completion must be persisted.\n- A completed activity must not be marked as reviewed unless a review exists.\n- Changing the baby\'s age must not silently rewrite historical records.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA working daily learning journey with age-based activity selection and persistent completion states."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "There was an unexpected error. Finish what you were doing."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STEP 7 \342\200\224 Build caregiver video recording and submission\n\nBuild the video submission feature.\n\nThis is an important differentiator of LittleSteps.\n\nA mother should be able to record herself performing an activity with her baby or upload an existing video.\n\n## User journey\n\n1. Parent opens an activity.\n2. Parent reads the instructions.\n3. Parent performs the activity.\n4. Parent clicks \"Record Activity\" or \"Upload Video\".\n5. Parent records or selects a video.\n6. Parent previews the video.\n7. Parent can replace or remove it.\n8. Parent provides an optional note.\n9. Parent confirms consent.\n10. Parent submits for review.\n\n## UI requirements\n\nCreate a simple upload page with:\n- Large recording button.\n- Upload from device option.\n- Video preview.\n- File size and format guidance.\n- Upload progress.\n- Cancel and retry actions.\n- Optional notes field.\n- Consent checkbox.\n- Submit button.\n\nDo not request unnecessary permissions.\n\n## Submission states\n\n- Draft.\n- Uploading.\n- Submitted.\n- Under review.\n- Reviewed.\n- Changes requested.\n- Failed.\n\nShow clear status messages.\n\n## Data requirements\n\nEvery submission must be linked to:\n- Parent ID.\n- Child ID.\n- Activity ID.\n- Activity version.\n- Submission timestamp.\n- Video storage reference.\n- Consent record.\n- Review status.\n\n## Security requirements\n\nThis feature handles videos of children.\n\nTherefore:\n- Never make child videos publicly accessible.\n- Use private object storage in production.\n- Use short-lived signed URLs.\n- Enforce ownership and reviewer authorization on the server.\n- Validate file type, size, and content.\n- Provide secure deletion workflows.\n- Do not store real child videos in insecure public folders or local mock storage.\n\nIf production video storage is not configured, implement a clearly labelled demo mode and do not pretend that uploads are securely stored.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA functional and privacy-conscious video submission workflow, including upload progress and clear submission states."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STEP 8 \342\200\224 Build the reviewer dashboard\n\nCreate a separate dashboard for authorized reviewers.\n\nReviewers assess whether the caregiver followed the instructions for a particular activity.\n\nThey do not assess the baby\'s intelligence, health, or developmental level.\n\n## Reviewer dashboard\n\nDisplay:\n- Total pending submissions.\n- Submissions under review.\n- Completed reviews.\n- Recently submitted videos.\n\nCreate a review queue with:\n- Parent reference.\n- Activity name.\n- Submission date.\n- Age group.\n- Review status.\n- Priority, if applicable.\n\n## Review details page\n\nDisplay:\n1. Activity title and version.\n2. Original activity instructions.\n3. Safety guidelines.\n4. Submitted video.\n5. Optional parent note.\n6. Evaluation checklist.\n7. Reviewer comments.\n\n## Evaluation system\n\nEach activity must have its own rubric.\n\nExample criteria:\n- Correct setup.\n- Required materials used.\n- Instructions followed.\n- Correct activity sequence.\n- Safety instructions followed.\n\nCriteria and weights must be configured for the activity by an authorized content administrator or qualified reviewer.\n\nUse these ratings:\n- Met = 1.\n- Partially met = 0.5.\n- Not met = 0.\n- Not assessable = excluded from the score, with an explanation.\n\n## Score calculation\n\nCalculate:\n\nInstruction Match Percentage =\n(Weighted points earned / Applicable weighted points) \303\227 100\n\nIf there is insufficient evidence to assess the activity, mark it \"Not Assessable\" instead of generating a misleading percentage.\n\n## Review submission\n\nThe reviewer can:\n- Save a draft.\n- Submit the evaluation.\n- Add feedback.\n- Request another submission.\n- Mark evidence insufficient.\n\n## Parent result\n\nThe parent should receive:\n- Instruction-match percentage, where assessable.\n- Criteria-wise feedback.\n- Positive observations.\n- Areas to improve.\n- Reviewer comments.\n- Review date.\n\n## Security\n\n- Only authorized reviewers can access submissions assigned or permitted to them.\n- Every review must be recorded in an audit log.\n- Parents cannot edit published reviews.\n- Review changes must preserve history.\n\n## Expected output\n\nA complete reviewer workflow from pending video submission to published review result."
    }
  }
}
